# swift-cross-ui v0.8.0 GtkEntry app-styling fix (CUESYNC-9 round 19)
#
# Pinned to tag v0.8.0, commit a6d206370812e3b9edba259d167e848892c5013d (matches
# Package.swift's `exact: "0.8.0"` / Package.resolved). Applied by `git apply` to the
# *resolved checkout* at `.build/checkouts/swift-cross-ui` — the upstream dependency
# source is never edited in place in this repo; this file is the only checked-in copy
# of the change. Applied LAST, after interactivity -> gsk-renderer -> window-present
# -> container-hit-testing; its hunks (`runMainLoop`'s global CSS provider and
# `updateTextField`) are disjoint from all four.
#
# Root cause (specs/CUESYNC-9-findings.md §0.18). A `TextField` styled the way CueSync
# styles every one of them —
#
#     TextField("Project Name", text: state.$projectName)
#         .foregroundColor(colors.textPrimary)   // near-white, dark theme
#         .background(colors.inputBg)            // dark, drawn BEHIND the entry
#
# renders as a PALE box with invisible text. Two independent GTK behaviours combine:
#
#   1. GtkEntry draws its text in an inner `text` node which the stock theme styles
#      DIRECTLY. GtkBackend applies the app's `.foregroundColor(...)` to the entry
#      widget's own generated CSS class, and the theme's node rule wins for the `text`
#      child — so the app's text colour was silently discarded.
#   2. GtkEntry paints an opaque themed background OVER whatever `.background(...)`
#      drew behind it, so the app's dark field never showed.
#
# Net effect on a dark-theme app: near-white text on the theme's pale entry, i.e.
# nothing legible. Every numeric StepperField, the cue table's NAME column and the
# preset-name field read as EMPTY on Windows. The characters were being inserted
# correctly the whole time (verified: the `changed` signal fires per keystroke,
# 't' -> 'te' -> 'tes' -> 'test', focus on the entry's GtkText, `editable=true`).
# A RENDERING bug, not an input bug.
#
# Fix, in two parts — BOTH are required, and each was verified by pixel-sampling the
# rendered window rather than by inspection:
#
#   a. `updateTextField` clears the entry's own background via the widget's generated
#      CSS *class*. A class selector (0,1,0) outranks the theme's `entry` element rule
#      (0,0,1); a plain `entry { background: none }` in the global stylesheet does NOT
#      win against it, which is why the pale box survived several attempts to remove it
#      globally. With this, the field background becomes the app's own (measured
#      12,12,18 = the section panel, in place of the theme's 230,230,230).
#   b. The global stylesheet makes the inner `text` node inherit the colour and drop
#      its own background, so `.foregroundColor(...)` finally reaches the glyphs, in
#      every widget state (`:focus` / `:disabled` / `:backdrop` / `:hover` each fall
#      back to the theme's pale box if left unstyled).
#
# Added to the SAME global CSS provider that already neutralises theme styling for
# `list` / `list > row` / `.navigation-sidebar` — existing mechanism, no new
# dependency, no new API.
#
# Deliberately NOT touched: `border`, `padding`, `min-height`. Those feed GtkEntry's
# size request, and swift-cross-ui measures widgets to lay them out; changing them
# would move layout geometry, which is exactly the class of change CUESYNC-9 spent
# rounds 8-18 untangling. Paint only, never measurement.
#
# GOTCHA worth keeping: GTK's CSS parser rejected a `/* */` comment containing braces
# with "Theme parser warning: Unterminated block at end of document" and then silently
# dropped EVERY rule after it — which masked this fix entirely for several rounds and
# made correct CSS look ineffective. This stylesheet is deliberately comment-free; the
# rationale lives in Swift comments above the string.
#
# Idempotency: `scripts/patch-swift-cross-ui.sh` and the CI legs guard re-application
# with `git apply --reverse --check` before applying, so a second run against an
# already-patched checkout is a no-op rather than a failure.
diff --git a/Sources/GtkBackend/GtkBackend.swift b/Sources/GtkBackend/GtkBackend.swift
